<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
    <parent>
        <groupId>ru.ludwigandreas</groupId>
        <artifactId>common</artifactId>
        <version>${revision}</version>
        <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>messaging-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>Messaging Spring Boot starter</name>
    <description>
        The consumer half of the envelope outbox-spring-boot-starter produces: one canonical set of wire
        header names shared by both halves, a typed inbound envelope that reads them back, and one
        listener container factory builder that fixes the decisions three services had each made
        differently - ErrorHandlingDeserializer around key and value, DefaultErrorHandler with
        exponential backoff, deserialization failures registered non-retryable so a poison record goes
        straight to the dead-letter topic, RECORD acknowledgement, a dead-letter topic per source topic,
        the consumer-side dedup filter from idempotency-spring-boot-starter wired in when a deployment
        asked for it, Micrometer consumer metrics including lag, and a silence signal for a topic that
        has stopped delivering. Non-blocking retry topics are shipped and are per-topic opt-in, refused
        at startup for any consumer that declares it cares about ordering.
    </description>

    <!--
        The JDK baseline and the spring-boot-dependencies import are inherited from the parent pom,
        ru.ludwigandreas:common. Third-party versions are NOT: they live in ludwig-bom, imported below.

        ludwig-bom is imported rather than inherited on purpose, and ru.ludwigandreas:common stays the
        parent rather than ludwig-service-parent: a library built against a fixed
        spring-boot-starter-parent bakes that Boot line into itself and stops being usable by a service
        running a different one.
    -->
    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>ru.ludwigandreas</groupId>
                <artifactId>ludwig-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <dependencies>
        <!--
            ===========================================================================================
            THE DEPENDENCY DIRECTION - read this before adding a dependency here.

            outbox-spring-boot-starter depends on THIS module, for ru.ludwigandreas.messaging.api's
            header names. That direction is the only one that works: this module needs a KafkaTemplate to
            dead-letter with, not an outbox, and a mutual dependency would be a reactor cycle regardless
            of scope, because Maven's reactor DAG ignores scope.

            The consequence is that every non-optional dependency here becomes a non-optional dependency
            of every outbox consumer. That is why spring-kafka and web-core are optional below and why
            there is no dependency on anything else in the repository except audit-core, which outbox
            already has. Adding a non-optional dependency here changes outbox's footprint - check that
            first.

            THE FALLBACK, if that edge ever has to reverse: split a zero-dependency messaging-core out of
            ru.ludwigandreas.messaging.api - MessageHeaders, InboundEnvelope, DeadLetterTopics, all of
            which are constants and records with no Spring and no Kafka in their signatures - and have
            both this module and outbox depend on that instead. audit-core exists because exactly that
            split was forced there.
            ===========================================================================================
        -->

        <!-- The platform's audit envelope and sink. A dead-letter send and an exhausted retry budget are
             both events the trail should carry; audit-core rather than audit-spring-boot-starter, because
             where the trail is written is a deployment's decision and this module needs only the seam.
             Non-optional, and that is safe: outbox-spring-boot-starter already depends on it. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>ru.ludwigandreas</groupId>
            <artifactId>audit-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            Optional, and this is the deliberate difference from idempotency-spring-boot-starter, which
            makes web-core non-optional and says why. Nothing this module throws reaches an HTTP
            response: an unsupported event version is raised inside a deserializer on a listener thread
            and its destination is the dead-letter topic. The bundle and the ExceptionProblemMapper exist
            for the service that chooses to re-raise one of these from its own HTTP surface, and
            MessagingWebAutoConfiguration is conditional on web-core being present, so a broker-only
            deployment resolves neither. Making it non-optional would put web-core on the classpath of
            every outbox consumer - see the dependency direction above.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>ru.ludwigandreas</groupId>
            <artifactId>web-core-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            Optional, for the same reason: outbox-spring-boot-starter keeps spring-kafka optional so a
            REST-dispatching deployment does not carry a broker client, and inheriting it non-optionally
            from here would silently put spring-kafka on that deployment's classpath - which is exactly
            the classpath every @ConditionalOnClass(KafkaListener.class) in this repository keys on.

            A service adopting this module brings spring-kafka itself, as all three current consumers
            already do.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.kafka</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-kafka</artifactId>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            Optional: consumer-side dedup. The filter strategy is taken from the context by its
            spring-kafka interface, so the wiring needs nothing from here; what this dependency buys is
            reading IdempotencyProperties' claim mode at startup, so a TRANSACTIONAL claim without a
            container transaction manager is refused instead of silently reserving keys for work that
            rolled back. Compile-only in effect - MessagingDedupAutoConfiguration is conditional on the
            class being present.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>ru.ludwigandreas</groupId>
            <artifactId>idempotency-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!-- The envelope's payload is JSON and the shared deserializer is Jackson's. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
            <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Spring Boot autoconfiguration machinery -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-autoconfigure</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-configuration-processor</artifactId>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            The constraint annotations on MessagingProperties. The API only, not
            spring-boot-starter-validation: a library has no business dragging Hibernate Validator into a
            consumer, and Boot's configuration binding validates these only when the consumer has a
            validator of its own - at which point the annotations are already here to be read.

            OPTIONAL, unlike every other module in this repository that takes this dependency, and the
            reason is the dependency direction at the top of this file. Non-optional, it reaches every
            outbox consumer transitively - and jakarta.validation-api on a classpath WITHOUT
            hibernate-validator is worse than neither: it satisfies web-core's
            @ConditionalOnClass(jakarta.validation.Validator), which then tries to build a
            LocalValidatorFactoryBean and fails with a NoClassDefFoundError on a hibernate-validator class.
            That is not theoretical - it broke audit-spring-boot-starter's context, which reaches this
            module through outbox and had never had the validation API on its test classpath.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>jakarta.validation</groupId>
            <artifactId>jakarta.validation-api</artifactId>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Optional: consumer lag, dead-letter sends, retries, and the silence gauge. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
            <artifactId>micrometer-core</artifactId>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
            <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <!--
            A real Bean Validation provider, test-scope only. MessagingProperties is @Validated, and Boot
            builds a validator for it whenever jakarta.validation.Validator is on the classpath - which in a
            consumer means "when that consumer brought a provider". Without one here the context fails with
            NoProviderFoundException, which is the failure a consumer would also see if it put the API on its
            classpath without an implementation, so having it in this module's tests is what proves the
            optional-API arrangement actually works.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-testcontainers</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            A real broker. Everything load-bearing in this module is how a container behaves when a
            record cannot be handled - how many attempts, with what gaps, and where the record ends up -
            and none of that is observable without a broker that actually has a dead-letter topic to
            receive it. spring-kafka-test's embedded broker would prove the wiring compiles; a
            Testcontainers broker proves the record arrives.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
            <artifactId>kafka</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ArchUnit, for this module satisfying the KafkaRules group it contributes rules to. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
            <artifactId>archunit</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>ru.ludwigandreas</groupId>
            <artifactId>architecture-rules</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <!--
            The platform's container images, singleton containers and fixtures. One test-scoped
            dependency; the digests live in its images.properties, not here.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>ru.ludwigandreas</groupId>
            <artifactId>test-support</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <annotationProcessorPaths>
                        <path>
                            <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
                            <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
                        </path>
                    </annotationProcessorPaths>
                    <annotationProcessors>
                        <annotationProcessor>lombok.launch.AnnotationProcessorHider$AnnotationProcessor</annotationProcessor>
                    </annotationProcessors>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
                <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>

            <!--
                Failsafe, for the same reason idempotency-spring-boot-starter has it: the suite starts a
                broker and waits out a real exponential backoff, which is not something `mvn test` should
                do on every build of every module that depends on this one. Named *IT, run at `verify`.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>integration-tests</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>integration-test</goal>
                            <goal>verify</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
